' Transport — synchronous roUrlTransfer wrapper.
'
' Transport is the only store that touches Roku's networking primitives. Real
' requests go through a blocking HTTP client (roUrlTransfer async calls parked
' on a message port with Wait); tests inject a fake client so every store,
' Transport itself included, runs in the brs interpreter with no device calls.
'
' The client seam returns a raw result { ok, status, body, error }. Transport
' normalizes that into { ok, status, json, error }: it parses the body as JSON
' and treats an unparseable body on an otherwise-successful response as a
' failure. Stores read result.json and never care where the bytes came from.
'
' Helpers live as instance methods: Roku scopes user-defined global functions
' per component, so a bare global called from a method that is invoked from
' another component's code resolves to invalid (call of a non-function). Only
' the platform's own globals (CreateObject, Wait, ParseJson, ...) resolve from
' any scope; anything user-defined must be reachable through m.

const REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 15000

' The dedicated-request timeout ceiling for the one caller that can legitimately
' take tens of seconds (a full addon-collection sync against a slow account). A
' default-sized request would abort it partway, so it rides the long path.
const LONG_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 120000

' How many times a request is attempted before its failure is final. The device
' refuses NEW concurrent connections past a ceiling of roughly 4-6 (measured:
' sequential soaks are perfect while 8-wide bursts drop transfers), so a fetch
' that loses the race can simply be re-issued. Three attempts ride out a single
' collision without turning a host that is truly down into a long stall.
const MAX_TRANSPORT_ATTEMPTS = 3

' Backoff before retry N (1-based: the first retry waits one base unit). Short
' and linear — a refused connect costs milliseconds — so a recovery costs about
' three quarters of a second while a dead host still fails fast.
const TRANSPORT_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS = 250

' Random spread added on top of that backoff, so colliding clients stop retrying
' in lockstep.
'
' The collisions this rides out are the device refusing NEW connections past a
' low ceiling, and a refused connect is answered fast — which is precisely when
' a fixed backoff is worst. Every loser wakes at the same instant, spends the
' same milliseconds being refused again, and spends the next one the same way.
' A fixed backoff reproduces the stampede it is meant to break; jitter spreads
' the retries across a 150ms window so the ones that gave up at different moments
' come back at different moments too.
'
' Small on purpose. It has to be invisible next to the connect it is spacing out
' (milliseconds) while still being large enough to reorder retries, and it is
' added to a linear base that already grows with the attempt number, so by the
' last retry the total wait is still under a second and a dead host still fails
' fast.
const TRANSPORT_RETRY_JITTER_MS = 150

' Reads can retry negative transport errors. Mutations are never automatically
' replayed: even DNS/connect errors may describe a hop after a redirect. The
' account outbox retains uncertain writes for an explicit retry/reconciliation.
function TransportFailureIsRetryable(status as integer, method = "GET" as string) as boolean
    if method <> "GET" and method <> "HEAD" then return false
    return status < 0
end function

' Backoff before retry `attempt` (1-based: the first retry waits one base unit).
function TransportRetryBackoffMs(attempt as integer) as integer
    return TRANSPORT_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS * attempt
end function

' The real client. Serializes a body assocarray to JSON and issues a blocking
' GET or POST over roUrlTransfer. The transfer runs async and Wait parks this
' thread until its roUrlEvent lands: the synchronous GetToString/PostFromString
' entry points discard the response code (and roUrlTransfer no longer exposes
' GetResponseCode), while the async path surfaces both the body
' (roUrlEvent.GetString) and the status (roUrlEvent.GetResponseCode).
function CreateSyncHttpClient() as object
    client = {}

    ' One line per failed fetch, from the one place in the app that fetches bytes.
    '
    ' Every store in the app reaches the network through this client — catalog,
    ' meta, streams, subtitles, library, auth, the streaming server — and before
    ' this, each of them reported failure its own way, or not at all. Transport
    ' itself had no trace, so a request that died at the socket left
    ' nothing anywhere to say so. One line here covers all sixteen call sites at
    ' once, which is the only practical way to answer "did this fetch work".
    '
    ' Query string stripped before printing. Addon addresses routinely carry
    ' credentials — EndpointUrl exists specifically to keep a "?token=..." intact
    ' through request assembly — and a raw URL would put those tokens into the
    ' console log, and from there into any log pasted into a bug report.
    '
    ' Every non-2xx is logged, not just transport failures, so an HTTP 404 shows up
    ' alongside the connect errors. That means legitimately-missing responses are
    ' logged too (a subtitle provider 404s a language it does not carry), so some
    ' noise is expected; narrowing to `status < 200 or status >= 500` is a one-line
    ' change if that drowns out the real faults.
    '
    ' Wrapped in try/catch: a diagnostic that raised would be caught by the caller's
    ' own catch and reported as a fetch failure, turning the trace into the fault.
    client._trace = function(request as object, result as object) as object
        if result.status >= 200 and result.status < 300 then return result
        try
            ' Configured paths can contain secrets too, and low-level error
            ' strings can repeat the full URL. Log only the origin and status.
            print "[http] " + request.method + " " + TransportLogOrigin(request.url) + " -> status=" + result.status.ToStr()
        catch ignored
        end try
        return result
    end function

    ' Retry policy, reached through m for the same cross-component reason the
    ' request functions are; the decisions themselves are file-scope so they can
    ' be unit-tested without a device.
    client._retryable = function(status as integer, method as string) as boolean
        return TransportFailureIsRetryable(status,method)
    end function
    client._retryAfter = function(attempt as integer) as integer
        ' The jitter is added HERE rather than inside TransportRetryBackoffMs
        ' because that file-scope function is exercised by the brs interpreter,
        ' which has no Rnd. Rnd is a Roku global and resolves on device; the
        ' closure is only ever built by CreateSyncHttpClient, which the suite
        ' never calls (every store test injects a fake client). So the one place
        ' that needs randomness is exactly the one place that runs only on the
        ' device.
        return TransportRetryBackoffMs(attempt) + Rnd(TRANSPORT_RETRY_JITTER_MS)
    end function

    ' Shared roUrlTransfer round-trip for both timeouts. roUrlTransfer is not
    ' re-entrant, so each request builds its own transfer + port; the two
    ' request flavors only differ in how long Wait parks before cancelling.
    ' Lives on the client AA (not as a bare global) because user-defined globals
    ' resolve to invalid when a caller from another component invokes the
    ' request functions — anything user-defined must be reachable through m.
    client._attempt = function(request as object) as object
        transfer = CreateObject("roUrlTransfer")
        port = CreateObject("roMessagePort")
        fs = CreateObject("roFileSystem")
        identity = transfer.GetIdentity().ToStr()
        output = "tmp:/cc-response-" + identity
        inputFile = "tmp:/cc-request-" + identity
        result = {ok:false,status:0,body:"",error:"Request failed"}
        try
            transfer.SetMessagePort(port)
            transfer.SetCertificatesFile("common:/certs/ca-bundle.crt")
            transfer.EnablePeerVerification(true)
            transfer.EnableHostVerification(true)
            transfer.EnableEncodings(true)
            transfer.SetUrl(request.url)
            if request.headers <> invalid then transfer.SetHeaders(request.headers)
            transfer.RetainBodyOnError(true)
            limit = TransportResponseLimit(request.url)
            issued = false
            if request.method = "POST"
                payload = FormatJson(request.body)
                if Len(payload) <= 131072 and WriteAsciiFile(inputFile,payload) then issued = transfer.AsyncPostFromFileToFile(inputFile,output)
            else if request.method = "HEAD"
                issued = transfer.AsyncHead()
            else
                issued = transfer.AsyncGetToFile(output)
            end if
            if issued
                clock = CreateObject("roTimespan")
                clock.Mark()
                completed = false
                while clock.TotalMilliseconds() < request.timeoutMs
                    event = Wait(50,port)
                    size = 0
                    for each path in [output,output+".tmp"]
                        stat = fs.Stat(path)
                        if Type(stat) = "roAssociativeArray" and stat.size <> invalid then size = size + stat.size
                    end for
                    if size > limit
                        result.error = "Response exceeds the device budget"
                        exit while
                    end if
                    if Type(event) = "roUrlEvent"
                        result.status = event.GetResponseCode()
                        result.ok = result.status >= 200 and result.status < 300
                        if request.method <> "HEAD"
                            stat = fs.Stat(output)
                            if Type(stat) = "roAssociativeArray" and stat.size <> invalid and stat.size <= limit
                                result.body = ReadAsciiFile(output)
                            else
                                result.ok = false
                                result.error = "Response unavailable within the device budget"
                            end if
                        end if
                        completed = true
                        exit while
                    end if
                end while
                if not completed and result.error = "Request failed" then result.error = "Request timed out"
                if not result.ok and completed then result.error = "Network or HTTP failure ("+result.status.ToStr()+")"
            else
                result.error = "Request could not be issued"
            end if
        catch e
            result = {ok:false,status:0,body:"",error:"Bounded request failed"}
        end try
        transfer.AsyncCancel()
        for each path in [inputFile,output,output+".tmp"]
            fs.Delete(path)
        end for
        return result
    end function

    ' Bounded retry around a single round-trip. The measured failure is a device
    ' ceiling on NEW concurrent connections (~4-6): sequential soaks are perfect,
    ' 8-wide bursts lose transfers, and the app's parallel launch syncs collide.
    ' GET/HEAD transport failures may retry. POST is never automatically replayed
    ' because a failure can follow an applied write or redirect. An HTTP error
    ' is an answer and a timeout (status 0) must not be multiplied by full waits.
    client._execute = function(request as object) as object
        attempt = 1
        budget = request.timeoutMs
        if budget = invalid then budget = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS
        clock = CreateObject("roTimespan")
        clock.Mark()
        while true
            remaining = budget - clock.TotalMilliseconds()
            if remaining <= 0 then return m._trace(request,{ok:false,status:0,body:"",error:"Request timed out"})
            bounded = {method:request.method,url:request.url,headers:request.headers,body:request.body,timeoutMs:remaining}
            result = m._attempt(bounded)
            if result.ok then return m._trace(request, result)
            if not m._retryable(result.status,request.method) then return m._trace(request, result)
            if attempt >= MAX_TRANSPORT_ATTEMPTS then return m._trace(request, result)
            delay = m._retryAfter(attempt)
            if clock.TotalMilliseconds() + delay >= budget then return m._trace(request,result)
            Wait(delay, invalid)
            attempt = attempt + 1
        end while
    end function

    client.request = function(method as string, url as string, headers = invalid as dynamic, body = invalid as dynamic, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS as integer) as object
        return m._execute({
            method: method
            url: url
            headers: headers
            body: body
            timeoutMs: timeoutMs
        })
    end function
    client.requestLong = function(method as string, url as string, headers = invalid as dynamic, body = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m._execute({
            method: method
            url: url
            headers: headers
            body: body
            timeoutMs: LONG_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS
        })
    end function
    return client
end function

' Origin-only diagnostics keep API keys in configured paths/query strings out
' of logs. Strip URL userinfo as well, even if a non-addon caller supplied it.
function TransportLogOrigin(url as string) as string
    schemeAt = url.InStr("://")
    if schemeAt <= 0 then return "[endpoint]"
    scheme = LCase(url.Left(schemeAt))
    if scheme <> "http" and scheme <> "https" then return "[endpoint]"
    authority = url.Mid(schemeAt + 3)
    for each separator in ["/", "?", "#"]
        at = authority.InStr(separator)
        if at >= 0 then authority = authority.Left(at)
    end for
    userParts = authority.Split("@")
    authority = userParts[userParts.Count() - 1]
    if authority = "" then return "[endpoint]"
    return scheme + "://" + authority
end function

' Percent-encode a value for a URL path segment or query component. The
' platform owns the real encoder: roUrlTransfer.Escape percent-encodes per
' RFC 3986, including proper UTF-8 for accented characters, and every caller
' runs inside a Task node where roUrlTransfer is available. The byte loop is
' only the fallback for environments without the component (the brs interpreter
' in `npm test`), where ASCII values encode identically.
'
' Named PercentEncode while the Transport method below is EncodeQueryValue, so
' the two never collide: one name for both would make the unqualified call
' inside the method resolve back to the method. The method is deliberately not
' UrlEncode either — that is the deprecated roUrlTransfer method, this already
' calls its replacement, and a method of that name reads as a call to it.
'
' File-scope with that method forwarding to it, deliberately: stores must reach
' user-defined helpers through m, but the scripted test transport is a plain
' object and has no m.transport to call. Keeping the implementation here lets
' that mock forward to THIS function rather than carry a second copy that could
' drift from it and make the tests assert an encoding the device never
' produces.
function PercentEncode(value as string) as string
    try
        transfer = CreateObject("roUrlTransfer")
        return transfer.Escape(value)
    catch e
    end try

    result = ""
    HEX = "0123456789ABCDEF"
    i = 1
    while i <= Len(value)
        b = Asc(Mid(value, i, 1))
        if (b >= 65 and b <= 90) or (b >= 97 and b <= 122) or (b >= 48 and b <= 57) or b = 45 or b = 95 or b = 46 or b = 126
            result = result + Chr(b)
        else
            result = result + "%" + Mid(HEX, b \ 16 + 1, 1) + Mid(HEX, (b mod 16) + 1, 1)
        end if
        i = i + 1
    end while
    return result
end function

class Transport
    client as object

    sub new(client = invalid as dynamic)
        if client = invalid then client = CreateSyncHttpClient()
        m.client = client
    end sub

    ' Percent-encode a value destined for a URL. Stores that assemble a URL call
    ' this through m rather than reaching for the file-scope function directly,
    ' because a bare user-defined global is only resolvable within the component
    ' that declares it.
    '
    ' Renamed from UrlEncode, which was never a call to that method: the encoder
    ' here has always been roUrlTransfer.Escape. The name was the only thing
    ' wrong with it, and it is a name a static analyser reads as the deprecated
    ' roUrlTransfer.UrlEncode — a deprecation this file does not have, reported
    ' against a call nobody can find.
    function EncodeQueryValue(value as string) as string
        return PercentEncode(value)
    end function

    ' Stream discovery may wait on a provider cache check longer than a catalog
    ' request. A caller can extend that one JSON request without changing the
    ' default for search, metadata or manifests.
    function Get(url as string, headers = invalid as dynamic, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS as integer) as object
        return m.Result(m.client.request("GET", url, headers, invalid, timeoutMs),TransportResponseLimit(url))
    end function

    ' A GET whose body comes back as text instead of being parsed as JSON.
    '
    ' Only for the streaming server's HLS manifest, which is not JSON. Through
    ' Get a perfectly good playlist reads back as "invalid JSON response" — the
    ' one response whose text is the entire diagnosis would be the one response
    ' thrown away.
    ' timeoutMs is a parameter because the one caller that polls — the playlist
    ' readiness probe — wants a timeout of its own, and at the default 15s a dead
    ' server would let its four attempts stretch the player's wait past a minute.
    ' The default keeps every other caller's behaviour untouched.
    '
    ' headers is here for the one GET that must not read the whole response: the
    ' streaming server's torrent route serves a feature-sized body, so asking it
    ' for a byte range through a Range header is what turns a readiness check into
    ' 64KB instead of a buffered film. Kept optional so the probe's existing
    ' two-argument call is untouched.
    function GetRaw(url as string, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS as integer, headers = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m.client.request("GET", url, headers, invalid, timeoutMs)
    end function

    ' A HEAD — the response's status and headers, and no body by definition.
    '
    ' Raw rather than m.Result(), for the same reason GetRaw is: there is no body
    ' to parse, and running "" through Parse would manufacture an "invalid JSON
    ' response" failure out of a request that actually succeeded.
    function Head(url as string, timeoutMs = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS as integer) as object
        return m.client.request("HEAD", url, invalid, invalid, timeoutMs)
    end function

    function Post(url as string, body = invalid as dynamic, headers = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m.Result(m.client.request("POST", url, headers, body))
    end function

    ' Long-window POST for requests that may legitimately run tens of seconds —
    ' today the Stremio API's addon-collection sync, which can sit behind a slow
    ' account. Uses requestLong so the default timeout is never stretched for
    ' every caller.
    function PostLong(url as string, body = invalid as dynamic, headers = invalid as dynamic) as object
        return m.Result(m.client.requestLong("POST", url, headers, body))
    end function

    ' Build a full add-on endpoint URL from a stored address that may carry a
    ' query string (the user's add-on config, e.g. a ?token=... API key).
    ' The endpoint path is inserted BEFORE the query so the request hits the
    ' right resource with the user's args intact.
    function EndpointUrl(storedAddress as string, endpointPath as string) as string
        at = storedAddress.InStr("?")
        if at < 0 then return storedAddress + endpointPath
        return storedAddress.Left(at) + endpointPath + storedAddress.Mid(at)
    end function

    ' Normalize a raw client result into the transport result: parse JSON when
    ' the body is non-empty, and demote a 2xx with a broken body to a failure.
    function Result(raw as object, byteLimit = 2097152 as integer) as object
        result = {
            ok: raw.ok
            status: raw.status
            json: invalid
            error: raw.error
        }
        body = raw.body
        if body <> invalid
            if GetInterface(body,"ifString") = invalid then result.ok = false:result.error = "invalid response body":return result
            bytes = CreateObject("roByteArray")
            bytes.FromAsciiString(body)
            if bytes.Count() > byteLimit then result.ok = false:result.error = "Response exceeds the device budget":return result
        end if
        if (body = invalid or body = "") and result.ok
            result.ok = false
            result.error = "empty JSON response"
            return result
        end if
        if body <> invalid and body <> ""
            if not TransportBodyLooksHtml(body)
                parsed = m.Parse(body)
                if parsed = invalid
                    result.ok = false
                    result.error = "invalid JSON response"
                end if
                result.json = parsed
            else
                ' The add-on answered 2xx in HTML — its landing page for an
                ' unknown path, or a gateway page — not in the JSON this
                ' transport promises. Same verdict as a body that fails to
                ' parse, but without handing the markup to ParseJson: the Roku
                ' runtime prints "BRIGHTSCRIPT: ERROR: ParseJSON: ..." to the
                ' console for every malformed body it is shown, and the dead
                ' opensubtitles.strem.io subtitle endpoint answers its
                ' stremio/v1 path exactly that way on every play. A body that
                ' opens with '<' is never JSON, so skipping the parse loses
                ' nothing but the noise.
                result.ok = false
                result.error = "invalid JSON response"
            end if
        end if
        return result
    end function

    ' Parse is a guard around ParseJson: the Roku runtime throws on malformed
    ' input rather than returning invalid, and Transport must turn that into a
    ' clean failure instead of a crash.
    function Parse(body as string) as dynamic
        if not TransportJsonDepthAllowed(body) then return invalid
        try
            return ParseJson(body)
        catch e
            return invalid
        end try
    end function
end class

' Limits apply to decoded response files and again before parsing. Transfer
' cancellation is best effort; a temporary file can overshoot between polls.
function TransportResponseLimit(url as string) as integer
    path = LCase(url.Split("?")[0])
    if path.InStr("/manifest.json") >= 0 then return 262144
    if path.InStr("/subtitles/") >= 0 then return 131072
    if path.InStr("/stream/") >= 0 or path.InStr("/catalog/") >= 0 or path.InStr("/meta/") >= 0 then return 1048576
    return 2097152
end function

' Avoid giving the native parser pathological nesting. Brackets inside quoted
' strings do not contribute to depth, including escaped quotes/backslashes.
function TransportJsonDepthAllowed(body as string) as boolean
    depth = 0
    quoted = false
    escaped = false
    bytes = CreateObject("roByteArray")
    bytes.FromAsciiString(body)
    for each ch in bytes
        if quoted
            if escaped
                escaped = false
            else if ch = 92
                escaped = true
            else if ch = 34
                quoted = false
            end if
        else if ch = 34
            quoted = true
        else if ch = 123 or ch = 91
            depth = depth + 1
            if depth > 32 then return false
        else if ch = 125 or ch = 93
            depth = depth - 1
            if depth < 0 then return false
        end if
    end for
    return depth = 0 and not quoted
end function

' HTML is the one body this transport is guaranteed never to mean as JSON: the
' dead subtitle endpoint answers its stremio/v1 path with the add-on's landing
' page, and the Roku runtime prints "BRIGHTSCRIPT: ERROR: ParseJSON: ..." to the
' console for every malformed body ParseJson is handed — up to once per retry
' per play. Correct to skip the parser when the body clearly is not JSON (JSON
' can never start with '<'), and cheap: only the first non-whitespace character
' is inspected, so a large body costs one loop iteration.
function TransportBodyLooksHtml(body as string) as boolean
    limit = Len(body)
    if limit > 64 then limit = 64
    for i = 0 to limit - 1
        ch = body.Mid(i, 1)
        if ch <> " " and ch <> Chr(9) and ch <> Chr(10) and ch <> Chr(13) then return ch = "<"
    end for
    return false
end function
